THE OPERATOR'S RULING: decision 0109 was accepted on 2026-11-17 ("accept 0109 as recommended", #1088). The standing ruling of 2026-11-04 ("go with recommended on anything else that comes up", "and by hand") covers this round. It does not cover accepting a security risk, so each HIGH is fixed, not accepted. Each recommendation below is the one taken, and the controller records it on #1088.

THE FIXES (one commit per item, each with its tests and a removal control recorded in the commit message):

**M1. HIGH (both positions; 0109 ruling 7, 0071 rulings 9 and 10): the terminal's own tag still sets the scope of what it owes.**
- **The fault (reproduced through the real engine).** A protected body [draft (Work), judge (Gate)] reaches a durable success on A, with judge's whole record on A. Re-tag the success's `step` from judge to draft, remove its `covered`, and set its `checked_head` to B:
  - verdict.rs `Producer::step` (about :197-223) computes an empty owed prefix;
  - `matched` (about :288-309) ignores records outside that prefix and accepts the covered-free work terminal's own head;
  - decide records B, and ship takes SHIP-OK on the unreviewed head.
  The same holds for a work step before the first gate in a four-step body. The compiler admits a work-first protected sequence (phases.rs about :100). An unresolved selector also falls into `Effect` (about :198-205).
- **The fix, recommended, taken:**
  - **no row's tag sets an obligation.** The lawful terminal positions come from the compiled body and its ending rules. Every record of the attempt is reconciled against them, records past the claimed position included;
  - **a body that holds a gate decides on a whole gate record's head.** The head it decides on is the latest whole gate record's `checked_head`. The terminal's own `checked_head` is compared, and is never the head decided on. That is what the gate-then-work case already does, since its decided head stays pinned to the trusted record; this makes it the rule for every body with a gate;
  - a work-only body (no gate) keeps K3's rule;
  - an unresolved selector refuses.
- **Tests:**
  - both re-tag-to-pre-gate-work cases (the two-step and four-step bodies): refused, or decided on judge's A, never on B. Say which, and why;
  - an unresolved selector: refused;
  - the work-only, gate-then-work, resume and newer-final-gate cases, unchanged.
  **Removal control:** read the terminal's position from its tag again, and the first test decides B.

**M2. HIGH (correctness; 0109 ruling 7, 0071 rulings 9 and 10): a protected single gate decides without its digest.** Dispatch arms stamping for a protected single gate or an all-gate panel (dispatch.rs about :164-180; engine.rs about :3229-3232). `Producer::Effect` erases the compiled class, and verdict.rs (about :261-269 and :288-290) accepts an absent `covered`. So a protected single gate decides on `checked_head` alone when its digest is stripped. **Fix:**
- the single-body producer carries the compiled class;
- a body whose dispatch arms stamping owes the digest form it arms, and an absent one refuses;
- the work-only terminal rule stays.
**Test:** a protected single gate's success with `covered` removed: refused. **Removal control:** let `Effect` accept the absent digest again.

**M3. MEDIUM (correctness; 0109 ruling 7): reordered gate records go unseen.** Swap two distinct, complete gate checkpoints before a valid final gate success. The fold (fold/observation.rs about :58-95) builds the same map, so there is no latch, and the verdict walks compiled order and accepts. A record's journal order must agree with the compiled order of its steps, and a record out of that order refuses. A genuinely newer final gate still decides. **Test:** the reversed pair refuses, beside observation_tests.rs's newer-final-gate case (about :614-638), which still decides.

**M4. MEDIUM (both positions; 0034 ruling 6, 0071 rulings 3, 8 and 9): a member name with `/` hangs the engine.** A panel member or step name may contain `/`: bundle/parse.rs (about :291-305 and :331-339) copies keys as names. seat_record/validation.rs (about :177-202) puts the raw tag into a JSON pointer, and `SeatRecordError::settled_member` recovers it by splitting on `/`. carry/record.rs `append_settled` (about :206-230) then removes the wrong key, or none. That has three effects:
- the loop never ends: the adversarial position's three-member drive hung;
- the error text grows without bound;
- with siblings `a` and `a/b`, the valid `a` is removed and the refused `a/b` kept.
A recipe author reaches this with no journal access. **Fix, recommended:**
- the refusal carries the exact member as structured data, never re-parsed from a pointer;
- `append_settled`'s loop is bounded by the payload's own settled keys.
Not taken: refusing `/` in names at compile, which changes the recipe grammar for a bug a typed identity closes. **Tests:**
- members `a` and `a/b`, with `a/b` refused: `a` is kept and `a/b` refused by name;
- the three-member drive with `b/x` refused ends.
**Removal control:** split the pointer again, and the second test does not end within its bound.

**M5. MEDIUM (correctness; 0109 rulings 1 and 3, 0071 rulings 7 and 9): a held terminal skips the per-member path.** When the store lock holds back the initial failed or indeterminate terminal, fence.rs (about :92-100) saves the payload before member validation. The recovery path, engine.rs (about :923-937), lands it through `append_raw`, so on the first `SeatRecordError` it writes an indeterminate row with no settled members. **Fix:** the recovery path appends through the same per-member path as `append_settled`. **Test:** X4's three-member fixture under store-lock contention keeps the valid sibling's verdict and retry evidence. **Removal control:** `append_raw` again.

L2 stays the named bend from d1bf96970, with its follow-up (the correctness LOW). The INFO stays as it is: V3's nested subset read is scoped on `authored` for K5's historical admission, and the driver's live accounting is telemetry.

GATES: 0109a1.md's gates, unchanged. brokkr-core is touched, so the core mutants gate is pending for the controller. Any API snapshot growth needs its `Ruling:` line, so name it among the asks. If the box refuses a command, report it as blocked.

THE COMMITS: "<area>: … (decision 0109 slice 0109a1, #1088)", signed off, one per item. Never amend, never push.

THEN: verify runs the whole workspace suite. The review panel reviews the WHOLE unit, a0f9a05c3 to your head, against 0109a1.md's "Reviewers, first" and the r2 to r7 checks. It also checks the following, each reported as a test case:
